source: crates/hibi-cli/tests/snapshots.rs
expression: "help(&repo, &[command, \"--help\"])"
---
$ hibi suggest-evidence --help
exit status: 0
---- standard output ----
Ask the judge model which code units, among those matched by --in, decide whether a statement is true. The result is a list of units to read, never a finding: nothing is written to the store.

Needs TYPESAFE_API_KEY and git. The text of the candidate units is sent to the judge service. Only files that git tracks, or that are untracked and not ignored, are sent.

Usage: hibi suggest-evidence [OPTIONS] --in <glob> <statement>

Arguments:
  <statement>
          The statement to find evidence for

Options:
      --in <glob>
          Where to look: a locator glob on scheme "file" or "repo", for example 'file:src/**/*.rs'. Repeat for several

      --kind <kind>
          Consider only units of this kind. Repeat for several [default: function, method, class, struct, interface, trait, enum, type, variable]

      --limit <n>
          Print at most this many candidates
          
          [default: 10]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

Global options:
      --root <dir>
          Use this directory as the root (the directory that contains .hibi/) instead of searching upward from the working directory

      --format <format>
          Output format. Without this option the output is JSON when standard output is not a terminal, and text when it is

          Possible values:
          - human: Text for a person
          - json:  One JSON object

      --now <timestamp>
          Use this instant as the current time, for reproducible runs. RFC 3339 with any offset, for example 2026-11-20T18:04:11Z. A fraction of a second is dropped

      --no-cache
          Do not read or write the cache database. Results are the same with and without the cache
---- standard error ----
